1️⃣ What is Multithreading?
Multithreading is a feature in Java that allows multiple parts (threads) of a program to run concurrently to improve performance and CPU utilization.
Analogy: A restaurant (process) where multiple waiters (threads) serve different tables at once.

2️⃣ Process vs Thread

Process:

Independent program in execution.

Has its own memory.

Slower communication between processes.

Thread:

Smallest unit of execution within a process.

Shares memory of the process.

Faster communication (shared data).

Example: Chrome browser = process, Chrome tabs = threads.

3️⃣ Two Ways to Create Threads

(A) Extending Thread:

class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}
new MyThread().start();


(B) Implementing Runnable:

class MyTask implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}
new Thread(new MyTask()).start();


Java 8 shortcut (lambda):

new Thread(() -> System.out.println("Lambda Thread")).start();


Runnable is preferred because it separates logic from thread management and allows inheritance.

4️⃣ Thread Lifecycle
NEW → RUNNABLE → RUNNING → BLOCKED → TERMINATED

start() moves from NEW → RUNNABLE

run() executes

sleep() or wait() → BLOCKED

finishes → TERMINATED

5️⃣ Common Thread Methods

Method	Purpose
start()	Starts new thread
run()	Executes task in same thread
sleep(ms)	Pause for given time
join()	Wait for thread to finish
setPriority(n)	1–10, default 5
getName()	Returns thread name
isAlive()	Checks if thread is running

Difference:

start() → runs in new thread

run() → runs in main thread (no new thread)

6️⃣ Synchronization (Thread Safety)

Problem: Two threads updating same variable → inconsistent result.

Without synchronized:

class Counter { int count = 0; void increment() { count++; } }


With synchronized:

class Counter {
    int count = 0;
    synchronized void increment() { count++; }
}


Why: Ensures only one thread accesses the method at a time.
Prevents race condition (data inconsistency caused by concurrent access).

7️⃣ ExecutorService (Modern Thread Handling)

Example:

import java.util.concurrent.*;

ExecutorService service = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    int taskId = i;
    service.submit(() -> {
        System.out.println("Task " + taskId + " by " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    });
}

service.shutdown();


Key Points:

Reuses threads for multiple tasks.

Use submit() to run tasks.

shutdown() stops accepting new ones.

Use Callable + Future to return results.

8️⃣ Common Interview Q&A

Q1. What is multithreading?
→ Running multiple tasks at the same time.

Q2. Difference between process and thread?
→ Process is independent, thread is part of a process.

Q3. Two ways to create a thread?
→ Extend Thread or implement Runnable.

Q4. Why is Runnable preferred?
→ Allows inheritance and better separation of logic.

Q5. Difference between start() and run()?
→ start() → new thread, run() → same thread.

Q6. What happens if we call start() twice?
→ Throws IllegalThreadStateException.

Q7. What is synchronized and why needed?
→ Ensures only one thread accesses resource at a time, prevents data inconsistency.

Q8. What is race condition?
→ Multiple threads modifying shared data simultaneously, causing incorrect results.

Q9. What is ExecutorService?
→ Thread pool manager for efficient execution and reuse of threads.

Q10. Can you restart a thread once finished?
→ No, a thread cannot be restarted once completed.

